{"result": {"response": {"id": "gen-1788041602-dLc0JhxeTFsd4VLstHXs", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "The ANSI TIA 570 standard provides comprehensive specifications for structured cabling within residential installations, adapting commercial cabling principles to meet the unique demands of home environments. This standard outlines the essential guidelines and best practices for designing and implementing robust telecommunications cabling systems in homes, ensuring efficient and reliable transmission of data, voice, and video services.\n\nA key aspect of ANSI TIA 570 is the differentiation of services and the specification of appropriate cable types for various applications, including CCTV and internet connectivity. The standard recommends Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP) cables of categories 5e, 6, and 6a, as well as coaxial cables, for residential use. Coaxial cable is specifically addressed for CCTV systems and broadband internet applications, with the standard setting clear cable distance limits to maintain optimal signal integrity.\n\nANSI TIA 570 places significant emphasis on proper installation practices and the importance of preventive maintenance to ensure the longevity and performance of residential networks. It prohibits electrical extensions or exposed cables in circulation areas, promoting safety and a clean aesthetic. The standard also recognizes the necessity of strategically locating power outlets near service outlets for convenient device connectivity.\n\nThe standard further distinguishes between single-family and multi-family residences, tailoring its requirements to the varying complexities of each. It addresses specifications for service entry points and internal distribution networks, ensuring a scalable and resilient infrastructure. Central design elements include the mandatory requirement for a central distribution point and the adoption of a star topology for efficient network managem